    "created_at": "2024-10-21T22:21:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-10-21T22:22:42Z",
    "body": "Coming to this a little late, my understanding is that `:user-valid` and `:user-invalid` are basically `:valid` / `:invalid` that the user has actually interacted with. If that is correct, I wonder if it would be less cognitive overhead for authors and more forwards compatible if `ElementInternals` instead provided **a way to specify when a control has been interacted with**. Then the UA can infer `:user-valid` and `:user-invalid`, with the bonus that it naturally leads to more decoupled code for authors, and any future web platform feature that relies on _has interacted with_ state does not need to expand `ElementInternals` even more. In fact, then the UA can even infer this with some pretty straightforward heuristics if not set.\r\n\r\n@annevk said:\r\n> I think the value in supporting `:user-valid` / `:user-invalid` is the same as with supporting `:disabled` and others. In that the web developers that end up using the form-associated custom elements get a consistent API surface across them and built-in elements.\r\n\r\n+100. But not just that, but also it reduces the API surface authors need to learn to use and customize a new component — you write your valid/invalid styles once, and they work for any WC.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
